{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":39061,"ns":0,"title":"Argumentum ad baculum","extract":"Argumentum ad baculum (Latin for \"argument to the cudgel\" or \"appeal to the stick\") is a type of argument made when one attempts to appeal to force to bring about the acceptance of a conclusion. One participates in argumentum ad baculum when one emphasizes the negative consequences of holding the contrary position, regardless of the contrary position's truth value—particularly when the argument-maker himself causes (or threatens to cause) those negative consequences.","description":"Threat of force to make a conclusion accepted","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}